  "unit": "u07-directions",
  "title": "Rickshaw & CNG",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Hailing a rickshaw goes like this: call **এই রিকশা!** ('Hey, rickshaw!'), name your destination and ask **যাবেন?** 'Will you go?' — **ধানমন্ডি যাবেন?** In speech the destination often needs no ending at all. If the driver says **না**, or just shakes his head, he doesn't want that route — ask the next one.\n\nAddress drivers as **মামা** (lit. 'uncle', mother's brother) or **ভাই** 'brother' — friendly and respectful. Use the polite forms **যাবেন** and **থামেন**; plenty of people say তুমি to drivers, but আপনি is always safe.\n\nThe future of 'go': **যাব** = 'I / we will go', **যাবেন** = 'you (polite) will go'. 'By rickshaw' takes the locative: **রিকশায় যাব**.\n\n**Agree the fare before you get in.** In practice rickshaws and CNGs don't run on a meter, so ask **ভাড়া কত?** If it sounds high, say **ভাড়া একটু বেশি** and make a counter-offer with your Unit 6 haggling: **পঞ্চাশ টাকা রাখেন?** A smile and a fair number work better than a long argument.\n\nTo get off: **মামা, এখানে থামেন** 'Stop here, please'. You'll hear **এখানে রাখেন** (lit. 'keep it here') just as often — both are right. And **কতদূর?** asks 'how far?': **ধানমন্ডি কতদূর?**\n\nA **সিএনজি** is the green, gas-powered auto-rickshaw — faster than a rickshaw and better for longer trips. In Kolkata it's an **অটো**.",
